  "body": " The curve is shown in the graph below. Sketch the graph of .      Try plotting points. If you can't divide by , take a limit.      Pictures may vary somewhat; the important points are the values of the function at integer values of , and the vertical asymptotes.  We can begin by plotting the points that are easy to read off the diagram. Note that is undefined when . So is undefined at and . We shall look more closely at the behaviour of for near shortly.  Plotting the above points, we get the following picture:     Since is constant when is between -1 and 0, then also is constant between -1 and 0, so we update our picture:     The big question that remains is the behaviour of when is near -2 and 2. We can answer this question with limits. As approaches from the left , gets closer to zero, and is negative. So will be negative, and will increase in magnitude without bound; that is, . Similarly, as approaches from the right , gets closer to zero, and is positive. So will be positive, and will increase in magnitude without bound; that is, . We add this behaviour to our graph:     Now, we consider the behaviour at . Since gets closer and closer to 0 AND is positive as approaches 2, we conclude . Adding to our picture:     Now the only remaining blank space is between and . Since is a smooth curve that stays away from 0, we can draw some kind of smooth curve here, and call it good enough. (Later on we'll go into more details about drawing graphs. The purpose of this exercise was to utilize what we've learned about limits.)      "
