  "title": "(Optional) — Making Infinite Limits a Little More Formal",
  "body": " (Optional) Making Infinite Limits a Little More Formal   For those of you who made it through the formal definition of limits we give the formal definition of limits involving infinity:  Limits involving infinity formal    Let be a function defined on the whole real line. We say that   the limit as approaches of is   or equivalently    converges to as goes to   and write if and only if for every there exists so that whenever .  Similarly we write if and only if for every there exists so that whenever .  Let be a real number and be a function defined for all . We write if and only if for every there exists so that whenever . Let be a function defined on the whole real line. We write if and only if for every there exists so that whenever .      Note that we can loosen the above requirements on the domain of definition of  for example, in part (a) all we actually require is that be defined for all larger than some value. It would be sufficient to require there is some so that is defined for all . Also note that there are obvious variations of parts (b) and (c) with replaced by .  For completeness let's prove Theorem using this form definition. The layout of the proof will be very similar to our proof of Theorem .   Proof of Theorem .   There are four limits to prove in total and we do each in turn. Let .  Let and set . Choose , then for any satisfying (or indeed any real number at all) we have . Hence as required.  The proof that is nearly identical. Again, let and set . Choose , then for any satisfying we have . Hence as required.  Let and set . Choose . Then when we have Since , as required.  Again, the proof in the limit to is similar but we have to be careful of signs. Let and set . Choose . Then when we have Notice that by assumption both , so . Now since , as required.  This completes the proof.    "
