  "body": " The following notations are all used for the derivative of with respect to  while the following notations are all used for the derivative of at    Some things to note about these notations:  We will generally use the first three, but you should recognise them all. The notation is due to Lagrange, while the notation is due to Leibniz. They are both very useful. Neither can be considered better .  Leibniz notation writes the derivative as a fraction  however it is definitely not a fraction and should not be thought of in that way. It is just shorthand, which is read as the derivative of with respect to .  You read as prime of , and as dee dee , and as dee-by-dee of .  Similarly you read as dee dee at , and as dee-by-dee- of of at equals .  The notation is due to Newton. In physics, it is common to use to denote the derivative of with respect to time.     "
