  "body": " Suppose you want to show that a point exists where the function has a tangent line with slope . Find the mistake(s) in the following work, and give a correct proof.   The function is continuous and differentiable over all real numbers, so the mean value theorem applies. and , so by the mean value theorem, there exists some such that .   Where is differentiable?  The function is continuous over all real numbers, but it is only differentiable when . So, if we want to apply the MVT, our interval must consist of only positive numbers or only negative numbers: the interval is not valid.  It is possible to use the mean value theorem to prove what we want: if and , then is differentiable over the interval (since 0 is not contained in that interval), and is continuous everywhere, so by the mean value theorem there exists some point where .  That being said, an easier way to prove that a point exists is to simply find it--without using the MVT. When , , so . Then .  The function is continuous over all real numbers, but it is only differentiable when . So, if we want to apply the MVT, our interval must consist of only positive numbers or only negative numbers: the interval is not valid.  It is possible to use the mean value theorem to prove what we want: if and , then is differentiable over the interval (since 0 is not contained in that interval), and is continuous everywhere, so by the mean value theorem there exists some point where .  That being said, an easier way to prove that a point exists is to simply find it--without using the MVT. When , , so . Then .  "
