  "body": " How many roots does the function have?  You'll want to use Rolle's Theorem, but the first derivative won't be very tractable--use the idea behind Question .       Let's begin by noticing that the domain of is .  By inspection, , so has at least one root.  If , then is positive, is negative, is negative, and is negative. Therefore, if is in the domain of , then . So, has no negative roots. We focus our attention on the case .   . We would like to know how many positive roots has, but it isn't obvious. So, let's differentiate again.   . When , , so , so has no positive roots . Since is continuous and differentiable over , and since for all , by Rolle's Theorem, has at most one root in .  Since is continuous and differentiable over , and has at most one root in , by Rolle's Theorem has at most two distinct roots in . (Otherwise, for some values , so for some and some , but since has at most one root, this is impossible.)  We know , so the remaining question is whether or not has a second root (which would have to be positive). As usual, we can show another root exists using the intermediate value theorem. We see that for large values of , is negative, for example:   For positive values of closer to zero, we hope to find a positive value of . However, it's quite difficult to get a number that obviously gives . It suffices to observe that and . From the definition of the derivative, we can conclude for some . (If it is not true that for some , then for all . The definition of the derivative tells us that [since exists] ; the denominator is positive, so if the numerator were always less than or equal to zero, the limit would be less than or equal to zero as well. However, the derivative is positive, so for some .) Therefore, has a second root, so has precisely two roots.     "
