  "type": "Exercise",
  "number": "3.3.4.2",
  "title": "",
  "body": " Which of the following functions satisfy the differential equation ?    You can test whether a given function solves a differential equation by substituting the function into the equation.  (a), (d)  Theorem tells us that a function is a solution to the differential equation if and only if the function has the form for some constant . In our case, we want , so . So, the theorem tells us that the solutions are the functions of the form . This applies to (a) (with ) and (d) (with ), but none of the other functions.  We don't actually need a theorem to answer this question, though: we can just test every option.   , so , so (a) is a solution.   , so , so (b) is not a solution.   , so , so (c) is not a solution.   , so , so (d) is a solution.   , so , so (e) is not a solution.    "
