  "title": "The Error Behaviour of Newton’s Method",
  "body": "The Error Behaviour of Newton's Method  Newton's method usually works spectacularly well, provided your initial guess is reasonably close to a solution of . A good way to select this initial guess is to sketch the graph of . We now explain why Newton's method usually works spectacularly well, provided your initial guess is reasonably close to a solution of .  Let be any solution of . Then . Suppose that we have already computed . The error in is . We now derive a formula that relates the error after the next step, , to . We have seen in ( ) that for some between and . In particular, choosing , Recall that is the solution of . So We need to get an expression for . Subtracting (E2) from (E1) gives If the guess is close to , then , which must be between and , is also close to and we will have and and Even when is not close to , if we know that there are two numbers such that obeys:       (we'll see examples of this below) then we will have Let's denote by the error, , of our initial guess. In fact, let's denote by the error, , in . Then (E4) says In particular By now we can see a pattern forming, that is easily verified by induction Mathematical induction is a technique for proving a sequence , , , of statements. That technique consists of first proving that is true, and then proving that, for any natural number , if is true then is true. . As long as (which gives us a quantitative idea as to how good our first guess has to be in order for Newton's method to work), this goes to zero extremely quickly as increases. For example, suppose that . Then Each time you increase by one, the number of zeroes after the decimal place roughly doubles. You can see why from (E5). Since we have, very roughly speaking, . This quadratic behaviour is the reason that Newton's method is so useful.   (Example , continued)  Let's consider, as we did in Example , , starting with . Then Recalling, from (H1) and (H2), that is a lower bound on and is an upper bound on , we may certainly take and if, for example, for all (as happened in Example ), we may take too. While we do not know what is, we do know that (since and ). As we took , we have , so that and This tends to zero very quickly as increases. Furthermore this is an upper bound on the error and not the actual error. In fact (E6) is a very crude upper bound. For example, setting gives the bound and we saw in Example that the actual error in was smaller than .   (Example , continued)  Let's consider, as we did in Example , , starting with . Then As , we may certainly take . In Example , all 's were between and . Since (to three decimal places) the IVT (intermediate value theorem) tells us that and .  So and all 's and hence all 's lie in the interval . Since we necessarily have and we may take . So This tends to zero very quickly as increases.   We have now seen two procedures for finding roots of a function  the bisection method (which does not use the derivative of , but which is not very efficient) and Newton's method (which does use the derivative of , and which is very efficient). In fact, there is a whole constellation of other methods What does it say about mathematicians that they have developed so many ways of finding zero? and the interested reader should search engine their way to, for example, Wikipedia's article on root finding algorithms. Here, we will just mention two other methods, one being a variant of the bisection method and the other being a variant of Newton's method.  "
