  "body": "The false position (regula falsi) method  Let be a continuous function and let with and being of opposite sign.  As we have seen, the bisection method generates a sequence of intervals , with, for each , and having opposite sign (so that, by continuity, has a root in ). Once we have , we choose based on the sign of at the midpoint, , of . Since we always test the midpoint, the possible error decreases by a factor of 2 each step.  The false position method tries to make the whole procedure more efficient by testing the sign of at a point that is closer to the end of where the magnitude of is smaller. To be precise, we approximate by the equation of the straight line through and .     The equation of that straight line is Then the false position method tests the sign of at the value of where . So once we have the interval , the false position method generates the interval by the following rule. The convergence behaviour of the false position method is relatively complicated. So we do not discuss it here. As always, we invite the interested reader to visit their favourite search engine.   false position method  Set . If has the same sign as , then and if and have opposite signs, then     "
